1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,320
that you can make, which if you're in the mafia,

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:02,880
maybe you do.

3
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:04,960
Maybe people will believe that threat.

4
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:08,680
If I made these threats, you're not going to believe me.

5
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:09,680
It's so weird, actually.

6
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:10,320
Maybe you would.

7
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:13,320
But wow.

8
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:15,520
I'm afraid to break my legs.

9
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:18,960
Actually, he threatened, and you said he would never do it.

10
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:21,480
But use some imagination if you shouldn't.

11
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:25,440
You can come up with some very scary things.

12
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:29,120
A classic scary thing for a kid at summer camp to do himself

13
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:30,920
isn't even to hurt another person.

14
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:34,200
So go and put out a cigarette on yourself.

15
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:36,960
And then how do people treat you after that?

16
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:40,400
According to every movie and TV show about this kind of thing.

17
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:41,640
But it's believable, right?

18
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:45,040
If you saw a person put a cigarette out on himself,

19
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:48,160
how would you treat that person afterwards?

20
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:49,160
Yes, stay away.

21
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:52,040
And what if he said, loan me a pen now.

22
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:55,160
Or are you going to say, no, no, no.

23
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:55,720
Yes, sir.

24
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:56,360
Here you go.

25
00:00:56,360 --> 00:01:00,040
He burns himself with cigarettes.

26
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:04,240
So as to what you're thinking, well,

27
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:07,400
these are all interesting questions.

28
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:10,440
There's some game theory that is underneath here.

29
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:12,880
You might think someone who would inflict such pain

30
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:15,400
on himself, God knows what he would do to someone

31
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:16,880
other than himself.

32
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:20,000
If you'll burn yourself, what would you do to a stranger?

33
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:21,640
I don't want to find out.

34
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:27,000
So yes, whatever you say, sir.

35
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:28,360
So now let's go over a few games,

36
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:31,800
and then we'll switch over to some of the most popular games

37
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:35,440
in all game theory, and ones which I will also say

38
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:37,560
have some relevance to the real world, probably

39
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:39,080
a lot of relevance to the real world.

40
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:40,840
So let's start with the prisoner's dilemma,

41
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:43,080
one that you've probably seen, I guess,

42
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:46,360
media like five times in classes, prisoner's dilemma.

43
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:50,040
So as you know, the apostrophe is after the S,

44
00:01:50,040 --> 00:01:52,480
indicating that it's a dilemma of a plurality

45
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:54,480
of plural prisoners.

46
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:56,160
It's not a dilemma of a single prisoner.

47
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:59,240
It's a dilemma of a group of prisoners.

48
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:02,480
So a standard representation is something like this.

49
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:05,880
So you've got two people who can either cooperate

50
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:07,480
with each other or don't.

51
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:10,800
They can either cooperate with each other or don't.

52
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:15,080
And then, of course, if they both cooperate with each other,

53
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:17,640
they both get a good payoff.

54
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:19,560
If neither cooperates with each other,

55
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:21,640
they both get a bad payoff.

56
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:24,360
But then if one cooperates and one doesn't,

57
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:26,880
the non-cooperator gets the best payoff,

58
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:28,760
and the person that did cooperate

59
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:30,240
gets the worst payoff.

60
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:34,080
So it would be something like 0, 6, and 6, 0.

61
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:38,880
So this is the classic setup of the prisoner's dilemma here.

62
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:43,040
So notice that here, both are better off than there.

63
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:48,600
OK, all right, so if you end up here, you say, gee,

64
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:50,000
why couldn't we have been there?

65
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,840
And yet, these two diagonal ones are

66
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:54,960
what are generally called the temptation payoffs.

67
00:02:54,960 --> 00:02:57,840
These are ones that give a temptation to each player

68
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:00,160
to not cooperate.

69
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:03,080
Now, this is a game that is standardly

70
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:04,200
solved by strict dominance.

71
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:06,280
I think we talked about it a bit already.

72
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:10,560
So player one is better off cooperating when?

73
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:13,040
This is only, we only play the game once,

74
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:14,360
you never see the person again.

75
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:16,000
It's all assumed.

76
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,760
So when is player one better off not cooperating?

77
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:20,720
Always.

78
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:23,280
When is player two better off not cooperating?

79
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:24,800
Always, yes.

80
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:28,200
So if it is always better for both of them to not cooperate,

81
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:29,800
then the game theoretic prediction is,

82
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:31,640
there will never be cooperation.

83
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:33,800
And we'll end up there, even though there

84
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:37,440
is that payoff right there, right in front of us.

85
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:39,240
But we don't go there.

86
00:03:39,240 --> 00:03:40,520
And why don't we go there?

87
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:43,560
Because we don't act.

88
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:44,720
There is no we here.

89
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:46,320
Instead, there are two individuals

90
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:49,080
each getting to act separately.

91
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:52,080
So wouldn't society be better off at 5-5?

92
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:54,560
Or at least the sub-society.

93
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:56,560
Wouldn't these two guys be better off at 5-5?

94
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:57,600
Of course.

95
00:03:57,600 --> 00:03:59,520
But if each chooses individually,

96
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:02,880
there isn't any way for society to take an action.

97
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:08,040
And so instead, you get this bad result.

98
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:10,240
So of course, the classic case of the prisoner's lemma

99
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:12,600
involves actual prisoners.

100
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:15,000
So you probably heard the story.

101
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,600
So there's two people arrested for a crime.

102
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:19,960
And then they are usually in the story.

103
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:21,680
We say we put them in separate rooms.

104
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:24,480
And then the prosecutor goes to each one and says,

105
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:30,360
look, you can either go and admit that you can go and,

106
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:31,920
what's the right word?

107
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:35,080
You go and rat out your accomplice.

108
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:38,640
And if you do this, you'll get a reduced sentence.

109
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:40,880
And I'm offering the same deal over in the other room

110
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:41,400
right now.

111
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:43,560
So I just thought you ought to know.

112
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:45,760
And the prediction is that both people

113
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:51,360
will rat, will rat, leading to the non-cooperative result.

114
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:53,840
So there actually is a murder mystery called Murder

115
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:58,360
at the Mergin, written by under the pseudonym Marshall

116
00:04:58,360 --> 00:04:59,560
Jevons.

117
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:03,840
It's actually Ken Elzinga at UVA, who's the author.

118
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:05,360
But anyway, so he has a murder mystery

119
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:08,560
where two innocent men both confess to the crime.

120
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:10,320
And then the economist comes along and says, well,

121
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:12,120
of course they both confessed to the crime.

122
00:05:12,120 --> 00:05:13,720
There was a prisoner's dilemma.

123
00:05:13,720 --> 00:05:16,280
And then he goes and really solves the crime.

124
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:18,800
There's something contrived about the murder mystery

125
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:20,640
because then it leaves you wondering, well,

126
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:24,520
why does anyone ever go and use this method of interrogation

127
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:27,120
if it always leads to confessions from everybody,

128
00:05:27,120 --> 00:05:29,280
no matter whether they're innocent or guilty?

129
00:05:29,280 --> 00:05:30,360
It's kind of a dumb system.

130
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:36,560
In the real world, there's something more to it,

131
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:41,400
namely you would want each person, when they confess,

132
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:45,040
to give details, facts, that they could only

133
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:48,440
know if, in fact, they were guilty.

134
00:05:48,440 --> 00:05:51,000
So this is an important proviso if you're

135
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:52,920
using the strategy in the real world.

136
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:56,360
Otherwise, you might get innocent confessions.

137
00:05:56,360 --> 00:06:00,200
A sub-point on the game theory of torture.

138
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:03,440
So Roman philosopher Cicero has a very interesting discussion

139
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:04,520
of torture.

140
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:06,280
So in Roman times, torture is very commonly

141
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:08,160
used to get confessions out of people.

142
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:11,440
And what about the objection that people

143
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:13,320
will admit to anything under torture?

144
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:14,920
Cicero knows this one.

145
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:18,200
And he says, yes, well, only the dumb kind of torture

146
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:21,680
involves torturing someone until they say, I did it.

147
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:23,840
He says that the good kind of torture,

148
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:25,440
the kind of torture that Cicero says

149
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:28,360
is the admirable torture, involves inflicting pain

150
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:31,480
until someone admits facts they couldn't

151
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:34,680
know unless they were guilty.

152
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:37,880
All right, and that ends the set.

153
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:39,920
So there's some logic to that, anyway.

154
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:41,520
Yes, there is, of course, a downside.

155
00:06:41,520 --> 00:06:43,640
You might have to kill an innocent person in order

156
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:44,360
to get this.

157
00:06:44,360 --> 00:06:47,000
All right, so.

158
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,160
I'm always puzzled by these interrogations,

159
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:54,400
the scenarios in fiction, where someone's getting tortured,

160
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:56,200
and then they have an answer, and they just

161
00:06:56,200 --> 00:06:57,800
don't make up something.

162
00:06:57,800 --> 00:06:59,680
So I'd always be there just making up answers,

163
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:02,960
just to hope that Jack Bauer saves me in time,

164
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:04,120
or something like that.

165
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:09,080
Like, yeah, the bombs in Idaho, in Boise, in the McDonald's,

166
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:10,760
on Main Street.

167
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:14,360
And if that doesn't work, I'm like, oh, I messed up.

168
00:07:14,360 --> 00:07:15,200
OK.

169
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:17,960
Anyway, when I was being tortured, I got confused.

170
00:07:17,960 --> 00:07:20,840
All right, so there is the puzzle

171
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:24,480
of why people under duress don't make up, well,

172
00:07:24,480 --> 00:07:26,480
aren't that are liars, and don't just make up,

173
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:29,840
and especially want to make up a very time-intensive scenario

174
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:32,640
in order to, where they have to put in a lot of work

175
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:34,440
in order to verify what you're saying.

176
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:37,360
Say, look, my contact can only be

177
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:40,240
met in three months and a day.

178
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:44,080
You have to be at the North Pole to do it.

179
00:07:44,080 --> 00:07:46,760
And until then, you're like, sorry,

180
00:07:46,760 --> 00:07:49,800
that's where it's all raised.

181
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:51,320
Any case.

182
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:53,560
All right, so this is the prisoner's lemma.

183
00:07:53,560 --> 00:07:56,480
That is the standard case involving the legal system.

184
00:07:56,480 --> 00:07:58,160
But it's got a lot of other applications.

185
00:07:58,160 --> 00:07:59,920
So very often, we'll bring it up when

186
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:02,400
we're talking about public goods and externalities.

187
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:04,080
So with a public good, what is it?

188
00:08:04,080 --> 00:08:06,080
It's in the interest of any one person to say,

189
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:07,560
let somebody else take care of the problem.

190
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:08,920
I'm not going to worry about it.

191
00:08:08,920 --> 00:08:10,560
But of course, if everybody says that,

192
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:13,400
then the problem doesn't get taken care of.

193
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:17,920
You get a very bad result. It's often used for collusion.

194
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:21,720
So if you think about it, from a social point of view,

195
00:08:21,720 --> 00:08:24,760
is it good that we have the literal prisoner's lemma?

196
00:08:24,760 --> 00:08:27,720
If there's two people involved in a horrible crime,

197
00:08:27,720 --> 00:08:29,800
is it actually a good thing that we have a system

198
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:33,000
to make them confess?

199
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,320
Sure, it's a great thing.

200
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:36,720
So that's one where we should really

201
00:08:36,720 --> 00:08:38,640
be thinking about there being a third actor here.

202
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:39,920
Or like everyone else, all the people

203
00:08:39,920 --> 00:08:41,760
who don't want to get murdered would be the third actor.

204
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:43,800
And they would actually be benefiting greatly

205
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:47,120
from a system where people in murder plots

206
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:49,400
tend to go to that equilibrium.

207
00:08:49,400 --> 00:08:52,480
But anyway, for collusion, this is a standard story

208
00:08:52,480 --> 00:08:54,440
about oligopoly, for example.

209
00:08:54,440 --> 00:08:57,500
So in oligopoly, of course, all the firms

210
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:00,000
that are in the industry would like to go and,

211
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,960
if they would all cooperate, all reduce production.

212
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:05,680
It's like in OPEC, if we all go and cut back oil production

213
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:08,160
by 20%, then based upon what we know

214
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:09,760
about the elasticity of oil demand,

215
00:09:09,760 --> 00:09:12,720
this would increase total revenue in the oil industry.

216
00:09:12,720 --> 00:09:15,680
And then we'd all be better off.

217
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:17,360
So often used for collusion as well,

218
00:09:17,360 --> 00:09:19,760
even though there, of course, as we know,

219
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:23,040
the deadweight costs of collusion are there.

220
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:24,920
But from the point of view of the firms,

221
00:09:24,920 --> 00:09:27,000
why is the firms don't go and cooperate

222
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:28,080
with each other more?

223
00:09:28,080 --> 00:09:29,920
And prisoner's lemma's a standard story

224
00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:32,960
because right after you make the collusive agreement,

225
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:34,200
then the first thing you want to do

226
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:36,120
is call up all your customers and say,

227
00:09:36,120 --> 00:09:37,440
look, tomorrow there's going to be an announcement

228
00:09:37,440 --> 00:09:38,920
that the price of increased,

229
00:09:38,920 --> 00:09:40,680
but for my very special customers,

230
00:09:40,680 --> 00:09:44,180
my favorite customer in the world, John,

231
00:09:44,180 --> 00:09:45,020
that doesn't apply to you.

232
00:09:45,020 --> 00:09:47,320
You keep getting the good old rates.

233
00:09:47,320 --> 00:09:49,240
And after you've called all of your customers

234
00:09:49,240 --> 00:09:51,320
to tell them that their rates aren't going up,

235
00:09:51,320 --> 00:09:53,340
then you start calling all of your competitor's

236
00:09:53,340 --> 00:09:55,280
best customers and say, I've heard a rumor

237
00:09:55,280 --> 00:09:56,960
that your rates are going to go up.

238
00:09:56,960 --> 00:09:59,000
And I would just like you to know

239
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:00,760
that you do have plenty of other options

240
00:10:00,760 --> 00:10:02,380
for your petroleum needs.

241
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:03,480
And then you do that.

242
00:10:03,480 --> 00:10:05,120
But of course, if everybody does that,

243
00:10:05,120 --> 00:10:07,120
then nothing much happens.

244
00:10:07,120 --> 00:10:09,200
If you go and read about the history

245
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:10,800
of cartels in the United States

246
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:12,920
before the antitrust laws,

247
00:10:12,920 --> 00:10:15,000
it's very colorful and gets a lot,

248
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,320
a lot of it does involve a lot of empty promises

249
00:10:18,320 --> 00:10:19,480
to go and reduce production.

250
00:10:19,480 --> 00:10:21,240
And then the promises,

251
00:10:21,240 --> 00:10:23,620
the empty promises are broken very quickly.

252
00:10:25,320 --> 00:10:26,600
All right, so collusion.

253
00:10:26,600 --> 00:10:28,320
People often looked at voting like this.

254
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:30,400
Is that only we could go and get,

255
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:32,120
wouldn't it be better if everybody voted?

256
00:10:32,120 --> 00:10:34,760
That one's a little bit odd because we'll,

257
00:10:34,760 --> 00:10:38,760
what if it just kept the result the same?

258
00:10:38,760 --> 00:10:39,880
There wouldn't just be more people

259
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:41,800
have to take time off during their busy day

260
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:43,000
to get the same result.

261
00:10:43,960 --> 00:10:48,840
But things like if there's a small interest group

262
00:10:48,840 --> 00:10:49,880
that is well organized

263
00:10:49,880 --> 00:10:51,600
